            <ArticleTitle>Identifying Trypanosoma cruzi discreet typing units in triatomines collected in different natural regions of Perú.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>167-179</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.7705/biomedica.v37i0.3559</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Trypanosoma cruzi has been divided by international consensus into six discrete typing units (DTU): TcI, TcII, TcIII, TcIV, TcV y TcVI. The factors determining the dynamics of T. cruzi genotypes vector transmission of Chagas' disease in the different geographical regions of Perú are still unknown.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To detect and type T. cruzi DTUs from the faeces of seven species of triatomines (Panstrongylus chinai, P. geniculatus, P. herreri, Rhodnius robustus, R. pictipes, Triatoma carrioni and T. infestans) captured in eight departments from different natural regions of Perú.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We examined 197 insects for detecting trypanosomes. DNA was extracted from each insect intestinal contents and PCR amplification of kDNA, SL-IR, 24Sα rRNA and 18Sα RNA was performed for detecting T. cruzi DTUs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Five T. rangeli and 113 T. cruzi infections were detected; 95 of the latter were identified as TcI (two in P. chinai, one in P. geniculatus, 68 in P. herreri, four in R. pictipes, seven in R. robustus, one in T. carrioni, 12 in T. infestans), five as TcII (four in P. herreri, one in T. infestans), four as TcIII (three in P. herreri, one in R. robustus) and four TcIV infections in P. herreri.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This is the first study which has attempted a large-scale characterization of T. cruzi found in the intestine of epidemiologically important vectors in Perú, thus providing basic information that will facilitate a better understanding of the dynamics of T. cruzi vector transmission in Perú.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
